I have read the whole codebase. This is a small, coherent local-first Electron app: one SQLite database, one preload bridge (`window.api`), and a React renderer for the dashboard, logbook, trades, savings, habits, tasks, calendar, review, search and settings. The good parts are real and strong, and the recurring-task work in `tasks.ts` / `tests/tasks.ts` / the backup layer already does most of what the contract asks for. I will write the explicit contract, add the remaining focused edge-case tests (rapid/done-undone-done completion, parent-edit after child exists, and a dangerous import with a dangling parent), then run the full check.

**Architecture in one paragraph:** main process owns the DB and every mutation; shared pure functions (`src/shared/`) implement the actual business rules and are tested; renderer is a thin view layer that calls `window.api` and re-renders. That split is clean and the tests follow it — `tests/` uses a real in-memory `better-sqlite3` database through the same main-process functions the app uses, which is exactly right for this codebase.

**Strengths worth keeping:**
- Pure-business-logic-in-shared + tests-in-tests + main-only-fetches/saves is consistently followed across habits, trades, savings, review, calendar, capture, palette, tools, search and now tasks. That is unusual to see this well applied in a small app.
- Recurrence is handled with real data-layer enforcement: per-row completion re-checks for an existing child inside the same transaction, and migration 7 adds `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_tasks_parent_once ON tasks(parent_task_id) WHERE parent_task_id IS NOT NULL`. The contract goal “completing again must never create a second child” is actually enforced at the DB level, not just the UI.
- Backup validation is the right shape: `parseBackup` in `src/shared/backup.ts` runs before anything is touched, throws readable messages for the first problem, and the import path tests the dangerous cases (dangling parent, self-parent, two children per parent, overdraw, duplicate check-in, bad schema version).
- Date handling is deliberate and consistent: calendar dates are `YYYY-MM-DD` text, habit/calendar/review arithmetic is UTC-based so time zones do not shift a day, and `localDate()` is used where “the user’s today” matters (dashboard tile, backup file name, completion date fallback).
- Security/escape-hatch posture is good for a local app: `contextIsolation: true`, the renderer never touches Node/SQLite, `isWebUrl` gates external links, single-instance lock prevents two copies fighting the DB/shortcut, and the import makes a safety copy before replacing data.

